WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.060
Who is "The Prince That Was Promised"?

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:07.330
No promises, but "House of the Dragon" might
finally answer that burning question.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:12.469
In the wake of its series finale in May of
2019, HBO's "Game of Thrones" left a lot of

00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:14.150
unanswered questions.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.850
One of the most pressing was regarding one
of the show's many prophecies, which were

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.789
presented throughout the season only to be
dropped by the finale.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:25.769
Among those prophecies, one of the most debated
was the one concerning the "Prince That Was

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:30.390
Promised," also known as the reincarnation
of legendary warrior Azor Ahai.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.630
"Game of Thrones," through the Red Priestess
Melisandre, spends a fair amount of time bringing

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:40.940
up the mysterious figure, and there are any
number of possible candidates for the role,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.480
including Jon Snow and his presumed half-sister
but actual cousin Arya Stark.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.230
We never get an answer, though.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.210
When the series ends, so does speculation
about this "Prince That Was Promised."

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.730
Now, the prequel series "House of the Dragon"
might have an answer in the weeks ahead.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.471
"Before Aegon's death, the last of the Valyrian
pyromancers hid his song in the steel."

00:01:02.471 --> 00:01:07.230
In the show's first episode, when King Viserys
decides to name his eldest child and only

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:12.400
daughter Rhaenyra as his official heir, he
tells her that Aegon the Conqueror predicted

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.371
a long winter that only the Targaryens could
fight — which he referred to as the "Song

00:01:17.371 --> 00:01:18.530
of Ice and Fire."

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:24.460
Then, in Episode 4, Viserys shows Rhaenyra
a dagger resting in the fire with words inlaid

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.450
upon it that also refer to the "Prince That
Was Promised."

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:36.757
"From my blood, come the Prince That Was Promised,
and his will be the Song of Ice and Fire."

00:01:36.757 --> 00:01:40.830
What this all means — and how it relates
to "Game of Thrones" — is definitely up

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:41.830
for debate.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:45.860
As fans of the original books know, the word
for "prince" in High Valyrian — the language

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.180
in which the prophecy is written on that very
dagger — is gender-neutral, meaning the

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.820
term could refer to a man or a woman.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.190
With that in mind, could Arya, who slays the
legendary Night King at the end of "Game of

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.280
Thrones," be this foretold prince?

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.930
However, this dagger seems to specify that
the prince or princess will be descended from

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.110
Aegon the Conqueror; Arya isn't, but Jon Snow
is.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:13.000
To further complicate matters, the dagger
is the very same Valyrian steel dagger Arya

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.590
uses to kill the Night King.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.760
Fans will likely never stop debating the identity
and mere existence of the "Prince That Was

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.850
Promised," and "House of the Dragon," at this
point, at least, is merely stoking the flames.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.459
When "Game of Thrones" premiered in 2011,
it quickly became the most talked-about show

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:34.690
on the air, drawing huge crowds and becoming
one of the last instances of "appointment

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.650
TV" during an era increasingly populated with
on-demand streaming hits.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.330
As its run continued year after year, the
show only gained traction, picking up major

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:50.170
accolades — including a whopping 59 Emmy
Awards — and gaining fans right and left.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:55.750
Then, in the spring of 2019, the finale aired,
and the chatter largely ceased, as one of

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.500
the world's biggest shows lost its cultural
capital seemingly overnight, thanks to its

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.230
controversial finale.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.450
We can go over what exactly went wrong with
"Game of Thrones," from dropped plot points

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.840
to ruined character arcs, until we're all
blue in the face, but there's no denying that

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.319
the series somehow ended with both a whimper
and a bang.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:19.920
"House of the Dragon," a big bet for HBO after
the outcry over the finale and final season

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.819
of "Thrones," has paid off so far, and has
even been renewed for a second season.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:29.530
And it does have a major advantage over its
predecessor in that its source material has

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.780
an endpoint clearly marked.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.880
Original showrunners David Benioff and D.B.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.410
Weiss had to move past George R.R.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:40.989
Martin's source material to wrap up the show,
whereas Martin has already written all of

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.080
"Fire and Blood," the source material for
"House of the Dragon."

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.820
Hopefully, "House of the Dragon" won't leave
us hanging like the original did, but truly,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.077
only time will tell.

